{%- doc -%}
  Pulls a single "Label: value" line out of the product description.

  Used by the PDP Redo spec table for the Colour and Design rows, which have no
  metafield of their own — the value is read straight from the Specifications
  block that every description already carries:

      Specifications:
      Material: Soft velvet upholstery, durable matte black metal frame
      Colour: Cream (seat and backrest), Matte black (frame)
      Design: Curved backrest, semi-circular base for added style and stability

  Returns the text after the label up to the end of that line, with the bold
  tags and surrounding whitespace stripped. Returns nothing when the label is
  absent, so the caller can drop the row instead of rendering an empty one.

  @param {object} product - the product object
  @param {string} labels - comma-separated aliases to try in order, without the
    colon, e.g. "colour,color". Matching is case-insensitive.

  @example
    {%- capture colour -%}
      {%- render 'pdp-redo-description-label', product: product, labels: 'colour,color' -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}

  WHY THE LINE BREAKS ARE REPLACED BEFORE THE TAGS ARE STRIPPED
  -------------------------------------------------------------
  strip_html on its own removes <br> and </p> without putting anything in their
  place, so "Colour: Cream</p><p>Design: Curved" collapses into one line and the
  Colour value swallows the Design line. Every line boundary is therefore turned
  into a sentinel first; the sentinel is what the value is cut at.

  The label is located on a lower-cased copy (Liquid's split is case-sensitive)
  but the value is sliced out of the original, so the reader sees "Cream (seat
  and backrest)", not "cream (seat and backrest)". downcase does not change the
  length of the string, so the offset carries across unchanged.
{%- enddoc -%}
{%- liquid
  assign result = ''
  assign source = product.description

  if source != blank
    assign text = source | newline_to_br
    assign text = text | replace: '<br />', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '<br/>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '<br>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</p>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</div>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</li>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</h1>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</h2>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</h3>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</h4>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</h5>', '~NL~'
    assign text = text | replace: '</h6>', '~NL~'

    comment
      strip_html takes the <strong>/<b> wrappers off both the label and the
      value, and removes any style="color: ..." attribute before the search
      runs, so an inline style can never be mistaken for the Colour line.
    endcomment
    assign text = text | strip_html

    comment
      Normalise the label punctuation before searching. Rich-text editors leave
      shapes that look identical on screen but do not match a plain "colour:"
      search: an &nbsp; entity or a literal non-breaking space between the label
      and its colon, which is what copy pasted out of Word carries. Both become
      a normal space, then a space in front of a colon is dropped, so every
      shape collapses to "Colour: value".

      The second replace below looks like it swaps a space for a space. It does
      not: the first argument is a literal U+00A0 non-breaking space. Do not
      "tidy" it away.
    endcomment
    assign text = text | replace: '&nbsp;', ' '
    assign text = text | replace: ' ', ' '
    assign text = text | replace: ' :', ':'
    assign lower = text | downcase

    comment
      A value that is not closed by a line boundary is cut at the next label
      instead, so a description written as one long line still yields one field
      per row rather than the whole Specifications block.
    endcomment
    assign stop_labels = 'Dimensions:,Specifications:,Specification:,Material:,Colour:,Color:,Design:,Style:,Assembly:,Care:,Weight:,dimensions:,specifications:,specification:,material:,colour:,color:,design:,style:,assembly:,care:,weight:' | split: ','

    assign label_list = labels | split: ','
    for label in label_list
      if result == blank
        assign needle = label | strip | downcase | append: ':'

        if lower contains needle
          assign needle_size = needle | size
          assign start = lower | split: needle | first | size | plus: needle_size
          assign value = text | slice: start, 500
          assign rest_lines = value | split: '~NL~'
          assign value = rest_lines | first | strip

          comment
            Some descriptions put the label on its own line with the value on
            the next one. Taking only what follows the colon returns nothing
            there and the row disappears, so fall through to the first non-empty
            line instead.
          endcomment
          if value == blank
            for line in rest_lines
              if value == blank
                assign value = line | strip
              endif
            endfor
          endif

          for stop in stop_labels
            assign value = value | split: stop | first
          endfor

          assign value = value | strip
          if value != blank
            assign result = value
          endif
        endif
      endif
    endfor
  endif
-%}
{{- result -}}
